    intro: ["Tyler is a city of about 134,739 people in Smith County, Texas. Coverage data shows water-damage restoration contractors serving 9 ZIP codes here — making it the 13th most-covered city for water damage restoration in Texas.", "When water gets in — a burst pipe, a room flooded after a Gulf storm, a leaking water heater — BayouDry connects you with an independent local restoration contractor who serves the Tyler area: fast on extraction, thorough on drying, and clear about the plan in writing before any work begins."],
    housingNotes: ["Coverage spans 9 ZIP codes across Smith County, home to about 134,739 residents.", "Like much of Texas, Tyler's housing is dominated by slab-on-grade foundations — when water gets under flooring, it spreads fast and hides. Brick veneer and composition roofs are the norm.", "Newer subdivisions tend to mean appliance and plumbing-supply claims; older neighborhoods more burst-pipe and roof-leak calls."],
    localIssues: [{ title: "Gulf storms don't stop at the coast", body: "Hurricanes and tropical storms push flooding far inland across Texas — not just the coast. Contractors serving Tyler see their busiest weeks after major Gulf weather systems." }, { title: "Clay soil and flat land", body: "Much of Texas sits on expansive clay with poor drainage. When it rains hard, water pools against foundations and finds its way in — which is why Tyler sees so many seepage and slab-leak calls." }, { title: "Freeze events burst pipes", body: "The February 2021 freeze proved even South Texas isn't exempt. Burst supply lines and water-heater failures remain a top claim driver statewide whenever temperatures drop hard." }],
